add("why-health-insurance-is-a-must", "Why Health Insurance Is a Must for Every Solapur Family", "Health", "health", "heart-pulse", "th-red", 6,
"Medical costs are rising every year. Here's why a family floater, plus a super top-up, is the smartest financial decision you can make.",
"""
<p>A single hospital stay for a heart procedure, dengue complications or a knee replacement can easily cost <b>₹2–6 lakh</b> at a good private hospital, even in Solapur. Most families pay this from savings, by borrowing, or by selling gold. Health insurance stops one medical emergency from wiping out years of savings.</p>
<h2>5 reasons you can't postpone health insurance</h2>
<ol>
<li><b>Medical inflation is high.</b> Hospital costs rise faster than general inflation. A ₹5 lakh cover that feels enough today may cover far less in 10 years.</li>
<li><b>Lifestyle diseases start early.</b> Diabetes, blood pressure and thyroid problems now appear in people in their 30s. Once you're diagnosed, buying a policy becomes harder and costlier.</li>
<li><b>Waiting periods are shorter if you start early.</b> Pre-existing diseases are covered only after a waiting period. Starting young means the waiting period is over before you need it.</li>
<li><b>Cashless treatment.</b> At network hospitals the insurer settles the bill directly, so you don't have to arrange money in an emergency.</li>
<li><b>Tax benefit.</b> Under the old tax regime, Section 80D allows deductions for health premiums paid for yourself, your family and your parents. The limits are higher for senior citizens.</li>
</ol>
<h2>How much cover is right?</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Family profile</th><th>Suggested base floater</th><th>Smart add-on</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Young couple / small family (under 40)</td><td>₹5–10 lakh</td><td>Super top-up of ₹10–25 lakh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Family with eldest member 45+</td><td>₹10–15 lakh</td><td>Super top-up of ₹25 lakh+</td></tr>
<tr><td>Senior-citizen parents</td><td>Separate senior plan of ₹5–10 lakh</td><td>Keep parents off your main floater</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Try our <a href="../calculators.html#health">Health Cover Estimator</a> for a quick suggestion.</p>
<h2>What to check before buying</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Room rent limits.</b> A cap can reduce your whole claim proportionately. Prefer "no room rent capping" or "single private room".</li>
<li><b>Co-payment.</b> Avoid it unless the premium saving is large.</li>
<li><b>Waiting periods</b> for pre-existing diseases and specific illnesses.</li>
<li><b>Restoration benefit</b> and <b>no-claim bonus</b>.</li>
<li><b>Network hospitals in Solapur</b> where you'd actually go for treatment.</li>
<li><b>Claim settlement experience</b> of the insurer.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b>Good news:</b> since September 2025, premiums for individual health insurance policies are exempt from GST, which makes cover more affordable than before.</div>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Health insurance isn't an expense. It protects everything else you've saved. Buy it while you're healthy, review it every few years, and add a super top-up as costs rise.</p>
""")

add("importance-of-lic-policy", "Why an LIC Policy Still Matters: Benefits Explained", "Life & LIC", "life", "shield-check", "th-blue", 6,
"LIC has been India's most trusted life insurer for decades. Here's what traditional LIC plans really offer, and how they fit with term insurance.",
"""
<p>For generations, an LIC policy has been the first financial product in most Indian households. Many people now ask whether traditional plans still make sense when term insurance and mutual funds are available. The honest answer is that <b>they serve a different purpose</b>, and for many families that purpose still matters.</p>
<h2>Key benefits of LIC traditional plans</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Sovereign-backed trust.</b> LIC is a Government of India–owned insurer. The government guarantee on policy money is a major source of comfort for conservative savers.</li>
<li><b>Forced, disciplined savings.</b> A fixed annual premium builds a savings habit, which suits people with irregular or business income.</li>
<li><b>Life cover + savings in one.</b> Endowment and whole-life plans pay a death benefit during the term and a maturity benefit with bonuses at the end.</li>
<li><b>Bonus additions.</b> Participating plans earn reversionary bonuses each year and a possible final additional bonus at maturity. These are declared by LIC and are not guaranteed in advance.</li>
<li><b>Loan facility.</b> After a few years you can take a loan against the policy at reasonable rates instead of breaking investments.</li>
<li><b>Tax benefits.</b> Under the old regime, premiums may qualify under Section 80C, and maturity proceeds may be tax-free under Section 10(10D), subject to conditions.</li>
<li><b>Nomination & settlement network.</b> LIC has a branch and agent network in every district, including Solapur.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Where LIC traditional plans fit and where they don't</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Best tool</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>High life cover for family income protection</td><td>Term insurance (LIC or private)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Guaranteed/low-risk savings with life cover</td><td>LIC endowment / whole-life plans</td></tr>
<tr><td>Long-term wealth creation (10+ yrs)</td><td>Equity mutual funds via SIP</td></tr>
<tr><td>Regular pension after retirement</td><td>LIC pension/annuity plans or SWP</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="note">Traditional plans usually give moderate returns (check the real yield with our <a href="../calculators.html#irr">Policy IRR Calculator</a>). The smartest approach for most families is <b>term insurance for protection + LIC for safe savings + SIP for growth</b>.</div>
<h2>Tips before you buy</h2>
<ol><li>First buy adequate term cover (10–15× your annual income).</li><li>Choose a premium you can comfortably pay for the full term. Surrendering early causes losses.</li><li>Always ask for the official benefit illustration.</li><li>Keep nominee and bank details updated.</li></ol>
<p>See our comparison of <a href="top-lic-policies.html">top LIC policies</a> to find which plan suits your goal.</p>
""")

add("top-lic-policies", "Top LIC Policies: Which Plan Suits Your Goal?", "Life & LIC", "life", "badge-indian-rupee", "th-orange", 8,
"A simple, goal-wise review of popular LIC plans: Jeevan Anand, Jeevan Labh, Jeevan Umang, Jeevan Lakshya, Jeevan Utsav and more.",
"""
<p>LIC offers dozens of plans, and choosing the right one can be confusing. Below is a <b>goal-wise review of the most popular LIC plans</b> people ask us about in Solapur. Plan names, features and availability can change, so always confirm the latest version and benefit illustration before you buy.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Type</th><th>Best for</th><th>Key highlight</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><b>New Jeevan Anand</b></td><td>Endowment + whole-life cover</td><td>Savings + lifelong protection</td><td>Maturity benefit, with cover continuing after maturity</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jeevan Labh</b></td><td>Limited-pay endowment</td><td>Medium-term goals</td><td>Pay for fewer years than the policy term</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jeevan Umang</b></td><td>Whole-life with survival benefits</td><td>Lifelong annual income</td><td>Yearly survival benefit after the premium term</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jeevan Lakshya</b></td><td>Endowment with family income</td><td>Child's future</td><td>Annual income benefit to family if the policyholder dies</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jeevan Utsav</b></td><td>Whole-life, guaranteed additions</td><td>Guaranteed lifelong income</td><td>Guaranteed additions and regular/flexi income options</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Tech Term / Digi Term</b></td><td>Pure term</td><td>Maximum cover at low cost</td><td>High sum assured, online-friendly</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>New Jeevan Shanti</b></td><td>Deferred annuity</td><td>Retirement pension</td><td>Pension rate locked in at purchase</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">1</span><div><h3>New Jeevan Anand: the classic all-rounder</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Participating</span><span class="tag o">Whole-life cover</span></div></div></div>
<p>It pays the sum assured plus bonuses at maturity, and life cover continues for the whole of life after that. That's why it's a favourite for people who want both savings and lasting protection.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Strengths</b><ul><li>Two benefits: maturity and lifelong cover</li><li>Bonus participation</li><li>Loan facility</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Consider</b><ul><li>Moderate returns</li><li>Higher premium than term for the same cover</li></ul></div></div></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">2</span><div><h3>Jeevan Labh: pay less, stay covered longer</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Limited pay</span><span class="tag o">Endowment</span></div></div></div>
<p>The premium-paying term is shorter than the policy term. This suits business owners and salaried people who want to finish paying during their earning years.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Strengths</b><ul><li>Limited premium years</li><li>Lump sum at maturity</li><li>Bonuses</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Consider</b><ul><li>Premiums are higher during the paying years</li><li>Lock-in until maturity</li></ul></div></div></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">3</span><div><h3>Jeevan Umang: income for life</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Whole life</span><span class="tag o">Survival benefits</span></div></div></div>
<p>After the premium-paying term, it pays an annual survival benefit for life, plus a lump sum at maturity or on death. It's popular as a "pension-like" plan for the family.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Strengths</b><ul><li>Regular income</li><li>Covers up to age 100</li><li>Legacy for children</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Consider</b><ul><li>Long commitment</li><li>Inflation reduces the real value of a fixed income</li></ul></div></div></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">4</span><div><h3>Jeevan Lakshya: securing your child's goal</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Child goal</span><span class="tag o">Family income</span></div></div></div>
<p>If the policyholder dies, future premiums are effectively not required, the family gets an annual income, and the maturity amount is still paid on time for the child's education or marriage.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Strengths</b><ul><li>Goal is protected in any case</li><li>Annual income to the family</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Consider</b><ul><li>Equity SIPs may grow faster for long horizons</li></ul></div></div></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">5</span><div><h3>Jeevan Utsav: guaranteed lifelong income</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Guaranteed additions</span><span class="tag o">Whole life</span></div></div></div>
<p>It offers guaranteed additions during the premium term and income options afterwards. It suits risk-averse investors who value certainty over high returns.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Strengths</b><ul><li>Guaranteed benefits</li><li>Flexible income options</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Consider</b><ul><li>Returns are modest, so compare the IRR</li></ul></div></div></div>

<h2>How to choose</h2>
<ol><li><b>Protection first:</b> buy a term plan for 10–15× your income.</li><li><b>Pick the goal:</b> child (Jeevan Lakshya), lifelong income (Umang/Utsav), savings + cover (Anand/Labh), pension (Jeevan Shanti).</li><li><b>Check the real return</b> with our <a href="../calculators.html#irr">Policy IRR Calculator</a> and estimate maturity with the <a href="../calculators.html#lic">LIC Maturity Estimator</a>.</li><li><b>Ask for the official illustration</b> before signing.</li></ol>
<div class="note">Plan names, features and availability are based on LIC's publicly listed products and can change. Kartik will share the latest brochure and benefit illustration for your age and term.</div>
""")

add("top-5-mutual-funds-review", "Top 5 Mutual Funds Review: Category-wise Picks for Long-term SIPs", "Investments", "investment", "bar-chart-3", "th-green", 9,
"A balanced review of five widely followed funds across flexi cap, large cap, mid cap, small cap and balanced advantage categories, with who each one suits.",
"""
<p>Choosing a mutual fund isn't about picking last year's top performer. It's about building a <b>portfolio across categories</b> that matches your goal, time horizon and comfort with risk. Below are five widely followed funds, one from each core equity category, that investors often consider for long-term SIPs.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Important:</b> This is an educational review, not a recommendation. Fund names, fund managers, expense ratios and portfolios change over time. Always check the latest factsheet and Scheme Information Document, and talk to your advisor before investing. Past performance does not guarantee future returns.</div>

<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Fund</th><th>Category</th><th>Risk</th><th>Ideal horizon</th><th>Suits</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Parag Parikh Flexi Cap Fund</td><td>Flexi cap</td><td>Very high</td><td>5+ yrs</td><td>Core holding for first-time investors</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>ICICI Prudential Large Cap Fund (earlier Bluechip)</td><td>Large cap</td><td>Very high</td><td>5+ yrs</td><td>Stability-seeking equity investors</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>HDFC Mid Cap Fund (earlier Mid-Cap Opportunities)</td><td>Mid cap</td><td>Very high</td><td>7+ yrs</td><td>Growth with moderate extra risk</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Nippon India Small Cap Fund</td><td>Small cap</td><td>Very high</td><td>8–10+ yrs</td><td>Aggressive, patient investors</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>HDFC Balanced Advantage Fund</td><td>Balanced advantage</td><td>High</td><td>3–5+ yrs</td><td>Conservative investors & SWP</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">1</span><div><h3>Parag Parikh Flexi Cap Fund</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Flexi cap</span><span class="tag o">Value-oriented</span></div></div></div>
<p>It's known for a disciplined, value-oriented style and a relatively compact portfolio. It has historically held some international stocks alongside Indian equities, and the fund house is known for transparent communication with investors.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Why investors like it</b><ul><li>Diversified across market caps</li><li>Long, consistent investment philosophy</li><li>Good as a single "core" fund</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Keep in mind</b><ul><li>May lag in sharp momentum rallies</li><li>Overseas exposure depends on regulatory limits</li></ul></div></div></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">2</span><div><h3>ICICI Prudential Large Cap Fund</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Large cap</span><span class="tag o">Top-100 companies</span></div></div></div>
<p>This is one of the largest large-cap funds by assets. It invests mainly in India's top 100 companies, which tend to fall less than mid and small caps in market corrections.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Why investors like it</b><ul><li>Relative stability within equity</li><li>Large, experienced fund house</li><li>Good anchor for any portfolio</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Keep in mind</b><ul><li>Large caps can be hard to beat. Compare with index funds.</li></ul></div></div></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">3</span><div><h3>HDFC Mid Cap Fund</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Mid cap</span><span class="tag o">Growth</span></div></div></div>
<p>It's one of the largest and oldest mid-cap funds. Mid-sized companies can become tomorrow's large caps, which gives higher growth potential than large caps, with more ups and downs along the way.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Why investors like it</b><ul><li>Long track record across market cycles</li><li>Well-diversified mid-cap portfolio</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Keep in mind</b><ul><li>Can fall sharply in corrections</li><li>Needs a 7+ year view</li></ul></div></div></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">4</span><div><h3>Nippon India Small Cap Fund</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Small cap</span><span class="tag o">High risk-high growth</span></div></div></div>
<p>It's among the largest small-cap funds, with a very widely diversified portfolio of smaller companies. It's best used as a <b>satellite</b> holding (10–20% of the portfolio) for long horizons.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Why investors like it</b><ul><li>High long-term growth potential</li><li>Broad diversification reduces single-stock risk</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Keep in mind</b><ul><li>The most volatile category</li><li>SIP is better than lumpsum here</li></ul></div></div></div>

<div class="fund-card"><div class="head"><span class="rank">5</span><div><h3>HDFC Balanced Advantage Fund</h3><div class="fund-meta"><span class="tag">Hybrid</span><span class="tag o">Dynamic asset allocation</span></div></div></div>
<p>It moves between equity and debt based on market conditions. That gives a smoother ride than pure equity, so it suits first-time investors, retirees and <a href="swp-monthly-income-from-mutual-funds.html">SWP</a>-based income.</p>
<div class="pros-cons"><div><b>✓ Why investors like it</b><ul><li>Lower volatility</li><li>Equity taxation with a hybrid approach</li><li>Good for SWP</li></ul></div><div><b>✗ Keep in mind</b><ul><li>Returns usually lower than pure equity in bull markets</li></ul></div></div></div>

<h2>A simple sample allocation</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Investor type</th><th>Flexi/Large</th><th>Mid</th><th>Small</th><th>Balanced Adv.</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Conservative</td><td>40%</td><td>10%</td><td>0%</td><td>50%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moderate</td><td>50%</td><td>20%</td><td>10%</td><td>20%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aggressive (10+ yrs)</td><td>45%</td><td>25%</td><td>20%</td><td>10%</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Taxation in brief</h2>
<p>For equity-oriented funds, gains on units held for more than 12 months are long-term capital gains. They're taxed at 12.5% above an annual exemption of ₹1.25 lakh. Short-term gains are taxed at 20%. Rules can change, so check with your tax advisor.</p>
<p>Estimate your goal with our <a href="../calculators.html#sip">SIP Calculator</a>.</p>
""")

add("sip-power-of-compounding", "How SIPs Grow Wealth: The Power of Compounding", "Investments", "investment", "trending-up", "th-purple", 5,
"₹5,000 a month can grow into a big corpus. Learn how SIP, rupee-cost averaging and step-ups work together.",
"""
<p>A <b>Systematic Investment Plan (SIP)</b> lets you invest a fixed amount in a mutual fund every month, starting from as little as ₹500. It's the simplest way for salaried people, shop owners and even students to build long-term wealth.</p>
<h2>Why SIP works</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Compounding:</b> returns earn further returns. Time matters more than the amount.</li>
<li><b>Rupee-cost averaging:</b> you buy more units when markets fall and fewer when they rise, which averages out your cost.</li>
<li><b>Discipline:</b> an automatic debit removes the temptation to time the market.</li>
<li><b>Flexibility:</b> increase, pause or stop the SIP at any time (except ELSS lock-in).</li>
</ul>
<h2>The cost of waiting</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Start age</th><th>Monthly SIP</th><th>Years till 60</th><th>Invested</th><th>Value at 12% p.a.*</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>25</td><td>₹5,000</td><td>35</td><td>₹21 lakh</td><td>≈ ₹3.2 crore</td></tr>
<tr><td>35</td><td>₹5,000</td><td>25</td><td>₹15 lakh</td><td>≈ ₹95 lakh</td></tr>
<tr><td>45</td><td>₹5,000</td><td>15</td><td>₹9 lakh</td><td>≈ ₹25 lakh</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p style="font-size:.85rem" class="muted">*Illustrative, assuming a constant 12% annual return. Actual returns will vary.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Step-up tip:</b> increasing your SIP by just 10% every year, in line with salary hikes, can dramatically increase your final corpus. Try the step-up option in our <a href="../calculators.html#sip">SIP Calculator</a>.</div>
<h2>Getting started</h2>
<ol><li>Define the goal: child education, house, retirement.</li><li>Pick the category based on time horizon (see our <a href="top-5-mutual-funds-review.html">top 5 funds review</a>).</li><li>Complete KYC once.</li><li>Automate the SIP and review it once a year.</li></ol>
""")

add("term-insurance-complete-guide", "Term Insurance: The Biggest Protection at the Lowest Cost", "Life & LIC", "life", "umbrella", "th-teal", 6,
"Why every earning member needs term insurance, how much cover to buy, and the riders that matter.",
"""
<p>Term insurance is the purest form of life insurance. If the insured person dies during the policy term, the family receives the full sum assured. Because there's no savings component, premiums are very low. A healthy 30-year-old can often get <b>₹1 crore cover</b> for roughly the cost of a monthly mobile and OTT bill.</p>
<h2>Who needs it?</h2>
<ul><li>Anyone whose family depends on their income</li><li>People with home, car or business loans</li><li>Parents planning children's education</li><li>Business owners who want to protect the family from business debts</li></ul>
<h2>How much cover?</h2>
<p>A common thumb rule is <b>10–15× your annual income</b>, plus outstanding loans and future goals, minus existing savings. Use our <a href="../calculators.html#term">Term Cover Calculator</a> for a needs-based figure.</p>
<h2>Useful riders</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Rider</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Accidental death benefit</td><td>Extra payout if death is due to an accident</td></tr>
<tr><td>Critical illness</td><td>Lump sum on diagnosis of listed serious illnesses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waiver of premium</td><td>Future premiums waived on disability or critical illness</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accidental disability</td><td>Income or lump sum on permanent disability</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ol><li>Hiding medical history or smoking habits. This can lead to claim rejection.</li><li>Buying too little cover to save premium.</li><li>Choosing a term that ends too early. Cover until at least age 60–65.</li><li>Not telling your nominee about the policy.</li></ol>
<div class="note">Premiums for individual life insurance policies are exempt from GST since September 2025.</div>
""")

add("fire-shop-factory-insurance-guide", "Fire, Shop & Factory Insurance: A Guide for Solapur Businesses", "General Insurance", "general", "flame", "th-orange", 6,
"Solapur's textile units, shops and workshops face fire, flood and theft risks. Here's how to insure your business properly.",
"""
<p>Solapur is known for its textile industry: terry towels, chaddars and powerlooms, as well as engineering workshops, beedi units and thousands of retail shops. A single fire or short circuit can destroy years of hard work. <b>Business insurance is the safety net that keeps your enterprise alive.</b></p>
<h2>Main covers for businesses</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Covers</th><th>Ideal for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Bharat Sookshma Udyam Suraksha</td><td>Fire & allied perils (assets up to ₹5 crore)</td><td>Small shops & units</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bharat Laghu Udyam Suraksha</td><td>Fire & allied perils (₹5–50 crore)</td><td>Medium factories</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shopkeeper package</td><td>Fire, burglary, cash, glass, liability</td><td>Retail & wholesale shops</td></tr>
<tr><td>Machinery breakdown</td><td>Sudden mechanical/electrical breakdown</td><td>Powerlooms, CNC, workshops</td></tr>
<tr><td>Burglary insurance</td><td>Theft with forcible entry</td><td>Shops, godowns</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marine / transit</td><td>Goods in transit</td><td>Traders, manufacturers</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Perils usually covered under fire policies</h2>
<p>Fire, lightning, explosion, riot and strike, storm, flood and inundation, earthquake (often as an add-on), landslide, bush fire, impact damage and missile testing, depending on the policy wording.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes</h2>
<ul><li><b>Under-insurance:</b> if you insure stock worth ₹50 lakh for only ₹25 lakh, claims are reduced proportionately.</li><li>Not declaring stock fluctuation. Use a floater or declaration policy.</li><li>Ignoring add-ons like earthquake, terrorism and loss of profit.</li><li>Poor record keeping. Keep bills, stock registers and photographs.</li></ul>
<div class="callout"><b>Free risk check:</b> Kartik can visit your shop or factory in Solapur to assess sums insured and suggest the right package.</div>
""")

add("vehicle-insurance-guide", "Vehicle Insurance Explained: Third-party vs Comprehensive & Must-have Add-ons", "General Insurance", "general", "car", "th-blue", 5,
"Third-party is mandatory, but is it enough? Understand comprehensive cover, NCB and the add-ons worth paying for.",
"""
<p>Under the Motor Vehicles Act, <b>third-party insurance is mandatory</b> for every vehicle on Indian roads. But third-party cover pays only for damage to others. For damage to your own car or bike, you need comprehensive or own-damage cover.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Third-party</th><th>Comprehensive</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Injury/death of third party</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td>Third-party property damage</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td></tr>
<tr><td>Own vehicle accident damage</td><td>✗</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td>Theft, fire, flood</td><td>✗</td><td>✓</td></tr>
<tr><td>Personal accident cover for owner</td><td>Mandatory add-on</td><td>Mandatory add-on</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>Add-ons worth considering</h2>
<ul><li><b>Zero depreciation:</b> full claim for parts without a depreciation deduction. Best for new vehicles.</li><li><b>Engine protection:</b> covers water-ingression damage, which is useful during the monsoon.</li><li><b>Return to invoice:</b> pays the invoice value on total loss or theft.</li><li><b>Roadside assistance:</b> towing, battery jump-start and flat tyre help.</li><li><b>NCB protector:</b> keep your no-claim bonus after a small claim.</li></ul>
<h2>Smart renewal tips</h2>
<ol><li>Renew before expiry to keep your NCB and avoid inspection.</li><li>Transfer your NCB when you sell your old vehicle and buy a new one.</li><li>Don't claim for very small damages if it would cost you a higher NCB.</li><li>Compare IDV (insured declared value), not just premium.</li></ol>
""")

add("marine-insurance-for-traders", "Marine Insurance: Protecting Goods in Transit for Traders & Manufacturers", "General Insurance", "general", "ship", "th-teal", 5,
"Whether you send towels to Mumbai or import machinery, marine cargo insurance protects your goods on the road, rail, sea or air.",
"""
<p>"Marine" insurance isn't only for ships. It covers <b>any goods in transit</b> by road, rail, sea, air or courier. For Solapur's textile exporters, agro traders and engineering units, a single truck accident or theft can mean a loss of lakhs.</p>
<h2>Types of marine cargo policies</h2>
<ul><li><b>Single transit policy:</b> covers one consignment.</li><li><b>Open policy / open cover:</b> covers all shipments over a year, up to a limit, with declarations.</li><li><b>Annual turnover policy:</b> premium based on yearly turnover. Easy for regular dispatches.</li><li><b>Inland, import and export</b> cover as needed.</li></ul>
<h2>Coverage options</h2>
<p>Institute Cargo Clauses (A) give all-risk cover with standard exclusions. Clauses (B) and (C) cover named perils at lower cost. Add-ons like theft, pilferage and non-delivery (TPND), and strike, riot and civil commotion (SRCC) can be included.</p>
<h2>Claim tips</h2>
<ol><li>Note damage on the lorry receipt or delivery challan.</li><li>Inform the insurer immediately and preserve damaged goods for survey.</li><li>Keep invoices, packing lists and transport documents.</li><li>Lodge a monetary claim with the carrier within the time limit.</li></ol>
""")

add("swp-monthly-income-from-mutual-funds", "SWP: Create a Monthly 'Pension' from Mutual Funds", "Investments", "investment", "wallet", "th-purple", 5,
"A Systematic Withdrawal Plan turns your corpus into regular monthly income, often more tax-efficiently than FD interest.",
"""
<p>Most people know SIP: investing every month. <b>SWP (Systematic Withdrawal Plan)</b> is the reverse. You invest a lump sum and withdraw a fixed amount every month, while the remaining money stays invested and keeps growing.</p>
<h2>Who should use SWP?</h2>
<ul><li>Retirees who want a regular pension-like income</li><li>People who receive a lump sum from gratuity, a property sale or LIC maturity</li><li>Parents funding children's monthly college expenses</li></ul>
<h2>Why SWP can beat FD interest</h2>
<ul><li><b>Tax efficiency:</b> each withdrawal is part capital and part gain, so only the gain portion is taxed. FD interest is fully taxable at your slab rate.</li><li><b>Growth potential:</b> the remaining corpus can earn market-linked returns.</li><li><b>Flexibility:</b> change the amount or stop it any time.</li></ul>
<h2>The golden rule</h2>
<div class="callout">Keep your annual withdrawal <b>below the fund's expected long-term return</b>, around 5–7% a year for hybrid funds. Then your corpus can last for decades and may even grow.</div>
<p>Example: ₹25 lakh in a balanced advantage fund with ₹15,000 monthly withdrawal (7.2% a year). Check how long your money lasts using our <a href="../calculators.html#swp">SWP Calculator</a>.</p>
<h2>Best fund types for SWP</h2>
<p>Balanced advantage funds, conservative hybrid funds and, for shorter needs, debt funds. Avoid small-cap funds for SWP because their volatility can quickly eat into the corpus.</p>
""")

